!!<*yamlover: $defs: chapter>
'K&R: the inline switch'
description: a multi-line flow token is json5p, not a third collection style
- |
  A flow token written **across several lines** — K&R braces — is not a third collection
  style. It is the one **concrete switch the surface can express on its own**: that node
  and its subtree are written in json5p, inside an otherwise yamlover document.
- !!<format: text/x-yamlover> |
  a: {                 # spans lines → concrete json5p, K&R layout
    x: 1,
    y: 2
  }
  b: {x: 1, y: 2}      # one line    → yamlover, repr yaml/flow
- |
  The layout is not a preference recorded beside the value — it is the consequence of
  which language the subtree speaks; changing concrete mid-document is what yamlover is
  for. json5p and not JSON, because JSON5 has comments and json5p adds pointers and
  anchors: the switch loses nothing plain JSON would drop. Everything json5p cannot hold
  — a `!!<…>` tag, a `!!set`, an omni value-plus-fields, a keyed+keyless mixture, a blob
  — falls back to block form, exactly as flow's own refusals do.
- |
  The switch is authored provenance, never identity: a reflow leaves the document equal
  ([one document, many materializations](::language:principles:many-concretes)). In a
  `.yaml` file a multi-line flow token is just flow — there is no json5p to switch to —
  and it re-emits on one line. In the editor the gesture is the newline itself: a comma
  keeps the next element on the line, Enter puts it on the next one — that newline is
  the switch; Backspace just past the closer joins it back.
